1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The ancient Chinese saw — a
dragon.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Northern Hemisphere, July: a red star
sits low in the south.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
In ancient China, it had another name:
Dahuo, “the Great Fire.”

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,000
These stars form Scorpius, and Antares
is its heart.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
But 3,000 years ago, China looked up and
saw a dragon.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,000
From horns to tail, it spans more than
70° — about seven fists at arm’s length.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Seven of the 28 lunar mansions, joined
into one dragon.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Two stars — led by Spica, Virgo’s
brightest star.

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The dragon’s throat.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Four stars, also called Tiansi — the
Four Celestial Steeds.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Three stars. The red one in the middle
is the Great Fire — the dragon’s heart.

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
A long tail, dipping into the Milky Way.

13
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Four stars at the tip of the tail.

14
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:16,000
At spring dusk, its horns rise in the
east. The dragon’s position tells the
season, and when to sow.

15
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
North: Black Tortoise. West: White
Tiger. South: Vermilion Bird.

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Korea: a star chart on banknotes.

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

